+### Morton-ordered Block Layout
+Morton-ordered Block Layout is a spatial organization scheme that orders Gaussian blocks using Morton (Z-order) curves to preserve geometric coherence and improve CPU cache hit rates [S2, unofficial]. It maps 3D block coordinates to 1D Morton order indices to store blocks contiguously in memory [S2, unofficial]. Using Morton ordering yields a 95.2% CPU cache hit rate and 0.10 GB/iter PCIe traffic, whereas random ordering drops cache hits to 42.1% and increases traffic to 0.45 GB/iter [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$ due to the sorting step. The Morton code computation is $O(N)$ [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the Morton codes and sort indices [G].
-- **Measured Performance:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Scaling:** Not given in the sources.
-- **Strengths:** Improves spatial locality for subsequent compression or rendering passes [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** The 10-bit quantization limits precision [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use:** When preparing data for texture-based storage (e.g., PNG) or when spatial locality improves cache performance in subsequent steps [S1, unofficial].
-- **When not to use:** If exact coordinate precision is required without quantization, or if $N$ is very large and sorting becomes a bottleneck [G].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \log N)$ due to the sorting step, where $N$ is the number of primitives. Bit interleaving is $O(1)$ per primitive.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ to store the Morton codes and the sort order indices.
+- **Performance:**
+    - **CPU Cache Efficiency:** In out-of-core rendering scenarios, Morton-ordered block layouts achieve a 95.2% CPU cache hit rate compared to 42.1% for random ordering [S2, unofficial].
+    - **Memory Bandwidth:** Morton ordering reduces PCIe traffic to 0.10 GB/iter, compared to 0.45 GB/iter for random ordering [S2, unofficial].
+    - **Compression:** Sorting by Morton code improves compression ratios when quantizing and saving data (e.g., as PNG) due to increased spatial coherence [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Maximizes spatial locality, improves cache performance, and aids compression.
+- **Limitations:** Does not account for GPU hardware-specific load balancing (addressed by Shuffled Morton Order) [S1, unofficial]. Sorting is a global operation, which may be costly for very large $N$ if done frequently.
 
 ## Background
-This method is used in the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting, specifically in implementations like `gsplat-mlx` [S1, unofficial]. It was introduced to optimize the storage and retrieval of Gaussian splat parameters by leveraging the Z-order curve properties of Morton codes [S1, unofficial].
+Morton Code Spatial Sorting is derived from the general concept of Morton codes (Z-order curves) for spatial indexing. In the context of 3D Gaussian Splatting, it was adopted to optimize the storage and traversal of Gaussian primitives. The specific implementation using 10-bit quantization and bit-interleaving for sorting splats is documented in the `gsplat-mlx` repository [S1, unofficial]. The application of Morton ordering to block layouts for out-of-core optimization of billion-scale Gaussian splats is detailed in the TideGS paper [S2, unofficial].
 
